  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Le Café de France is at 5 boulevard des Aliziers, on place Neuve, in the heart of the medieval village of Grimaud. The tourist office describes it as a brasserie in the colours of Provence, with homemade dishes. The menu features sharing plates (duck confit rillettes, stracciatella, fried squid), starters such as eggs mimosa or salmon gravlax, the butcher's cut, a beef burger, sea bass en papillote and octopus tentacles, with chocolate mousse or tiramisu for dessert, as well as a selection of cocktails.</p>

      <h2>With a dog</h2>
      <p>The Grimaud tourist office listing says "Animals accepted" among the services, next to the terrace, toilets and free wifi. The restaurant has 80 seats, including 60 on the terrace, spread over two rooms. The listing does not say whether dogs are admitted in the rooms or only on the terrace, nor whether there is a water bowl or a size limit. The tourist office's page on dog-friendly restaurants reminds visitors that, if the dog is well behaved and on a leash, it is accepted in most restaurants in the village, and advises mentioning the dog when booking; that page does not list Le Café de France among the addresses on the first two pages consulted.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the restaurant takes bookings by phone on 04 94 43 20 05; say you are coming with your dog and ask for a table on the terrace.</div>

      <h2>Hours, payment and access</h2>
      <p>The restaurant is open all year, every day from 9am to 11pm, with lunch and dinner service. You can pay by bank card or cash. The entrance is level and the floor is hard-surfaced, with a table accessible to people with reduced mobility. Parking is signposted nearby, and a bus stop is less than 500 m away.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Grimaud castle and the lanes of the medieval village are accessible on foot directly from the square, so you can extend the outing with your dog before or after the meal.</p>
